  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP: An upper-level ridge over the central United \nStates and an Eastern-Pacific trough over the PNW and British \nColumbia are acting to create SW flow aloft over Montana.\n\nTODAY: The ridge will begin exiting eastward ahead of the trough,\nwhich will dig over eastern Montana by tomorrow morning. Embedded\nwithin the overall trough is a smaller shortwave that will \npropagate NE from over Wyoming to western Montana over the course \nof tomorrow afternoon. The shortwave's enhanced pressure gradient \nwill lend itself to producing gusty SE winds. Peak wind gusts are \nexpected to reach 35 MPH tomorrow afternoon.\n\nTONIGHT INTO SATURDAY: The embedded shortwave will continue \npropagating eastward as the main trough digs over western Montana \nand the front passes by early Saturday morning. Frontal passage \nwill cause winds to veer W-NW and daily high temperatures to drop \ninto the mid to high 60s. It is uncertain whether wind gusts will \nremain elevated overnight behind the front. Regardless, gusts will\ndie down by late Saturday afternoon. A Lake Wind Advisory has \nbeen issued, valid from 8AM Friday until 9AM Saturday.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH MONDAY: The trough axis will exit to the NE,\nleaving SW flow aloft over Montana in it's wake. Low-level\nmoisture moving through this flow coupled with isolated pockets of\nvorticity may generate rain showers between late Sunday afternoon\nand late Monday morning south of the Missouri River and east of \nFort Peck Lake. An isolated thunderstorm or two may be possible \nduring this time, particularly in the southeast most portion of \nthe CWA. Currently, the bulk of QPF is anticipated over the \nYellowstone Valley, but confidence is low as the track appears to \nbe shifting SE and totals are decreasing quickly between the last \nfew forecasts. \n\nMONDAY NIGHT ONWARD: Forecast in general looks to become semi-zonal\nas SW flow aloft flattens. However, ensembles are in disagreement\nbetween minor ridging and troughing, which is reducing confidence\nin the forecast from Tuesday night onward.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nWinds for the next 24 hours are anticipated to be higher than \ncurrent model guidance indicates. The NBM 90th percentile has \nmanually been given more weight in the forecast.\n\n\nDON",
